00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.870
DAVE GAGEOT: With almost
500 sessions on Next 2019,

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.800
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.700
So if you didn't see
"Kubernetes and VS Code--

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.200
Zero to Deployed,"
then stay tuned,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.810
because here's the rewind.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.191
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.790
So what is it like to be a
developer in a Kubernetes

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.580
world?

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.780
Well, we've asked
developers, and they told us

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:26.570
it's frustrating.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.260
There's too much configuration.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.820
Debugging is a pain.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.150
Application now have
so many moving parts.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:38.280
Developers simply want to
spend more time in their code.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.090
At Next, we've introduced Cloud
Code, a set of integrated tools

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.630
that help you remove the
complexity out of Kubernetes

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.540
and spend more
time on your code.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.170
Today, I'm going to show
you Cloud Code for VS Code.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.570
The first feature that
comes with Cloud Code

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.150
is a visual cluster explorer.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.630
Most of the things you would do
with the kubectl command line

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.630
you can do more easily
with a graphical interface.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.510
You can browse the
[? workflows, ?] list the pods

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.880
and containers, you
can stream the logs,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.240
or get a terminal inside a
running container with just

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.430
a few clicks.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.800
One can also create a cluster.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.510
And because we want
Cloud Code to be open,

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:21.790
we support multiple
cloud providers.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.990
You can use Cloud Code
on-prem, in the cloud,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.460
or on your desktop.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.550
Creating a cluster
on GKE is so simple.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.640
With just a few clicks, I
start a multinode cluster

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:33.990
on Google Cloud.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.350
I can even install
Istio or Cloud Run

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.910
just by ticking a box.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.880
It's very useful for developers.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.620
Another feature that
Cloud Code brings

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.830
is robust samples to
help you get started.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.780
No matter the language,
you can say bye

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.730
to the empty page syndrome.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.000
Here, for example, I'm going
to create a Go Hello World

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.990
example.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:57.090
Each sample comes
with source code,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.700
Dockerfiles,
Kubernetes manifest--

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.270
everything is preconfigured to
easily get started and learn.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.470
Cloud Code also provides
full documentation

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:09.150
for Kubernetes manifest.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.660
You get tool tips,
completion, validation--

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.400
everything you need to write
those YAML files more easily.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.020
With Cloud Code,
it's also super easy

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.020
to deploy my code to
a Kubernetes cluster.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.990
I just have to hit
Cloud Code Deploy

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.080
and the IDE will tell
me when it's done,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.690
and it should give me the URL
to connect to my application.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.360
It's so easy.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.700
An even more powerful mode is
to use Cloud Code Continuously

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:36.690
Deploy.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.740
It will redeploy my application
automatically each time

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.740
I make a change.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.890
I change the code, save,
and boom, it's deployed.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.470
This way, I can really
just focus on my code

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.940
and see the results
very quickly.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.930
Now, let me show you how to use
Cloud Code on a more advanced

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.620
project.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.860
It's a guest book project
that I've just started.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.450
It's not fully done yet.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:03:00.230
I've got the source code and
some of the YAML manifests,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.982
but not all of them.

00:03:01.982 --> 00:03:03.690
I'm going to use
another powerful feature

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.720
of Cloud Code, which is
YAML Manifest Templates.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.060
It's super convenient
for beginners.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.250
Once again, it helps you avoid
starting from a blank page.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:13.750
OK.

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.840
I'm going to finish
those YAML files

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.090
and deploy my application with
Cloud Code Continuously Deploy.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:22.950
See, the IDE tells me when
everything is up and running.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:24.720
OK, but my
application has a bug.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.760
So let's talk about debugging--

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.377
a major pain point
with Kubernetes.

00:03:29.377 --> 00:03:30.960
When your application
has a bug, you'd

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.330
like to know what's going on.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.340
And it's really not that
easy with Kubernetes.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.770
You have to connect
to remote container.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:38.950
Not easy.

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:41.490
With Cloud Code, I can
use the tools I'm used to

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.100
and hit Debug in
VS Code and boom.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.750
Debug is automatically
configured

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.870
to connect to the container,
and I can see what's going on.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.340
I can add breakpoints.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.900
I can see variables.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.000
I can even fix the bug,
and the application

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.770
is automatically redeployed.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:57.480
It's almost magical.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.740
To sum up, we've seen how to
get started from code samples.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.170
We've seen how to create
and explore remote clusters.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:09.000
And finally, we've seen how
easy it is to deploy and debug

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:09.870
our application.

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.600
So how do you get Cloud Code?

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.060
Just get it installed
from the marketplace,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.579
either on VS Code or IntelliJ.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.810
It's that simple.

